feat: painted "Biggest" lockup in the hero headline

Replaces the word with the supplied brush artwork.

The headline was a list of three strings, one per line, so a single
word could not be swapped without breaking the per-line stagger. Each
line is now a list of parts, and a part can be marked as artwork rather
than type — the mechanism is general, so any other word could be
treated the same way later.

The mark is sized in em against the headline, not in its own clamp, so
it tracks the type at every breakpoint automatically. Negative margins
pull the brush's transparent padding back in, which keeps the lockup
sitting on the line where the word did instead of pushing the line
wider. At 1440 it renders 508px against a 68px cap height; at 425 the
line wraps and the mark takes a row of its own, which reads better than
squeezing it beside "Africa's".

The accessible sentence is assembled from the parts and read once, so
"Africa's biggest web3 media brand." is unchanged for a screen reader
and for search despite one word now being an image. The mark itself is
alt="" — it is the word it replaces, not additional content.

next/image with priority rather than a plain img: this is part of the
largest element on the page, so it is fetched with the document. The
asset is resized to 1200px and encoded as webp with alpha, 132KB
against the 1.3MB png.
